$ABI strict
$Module cachetag 3 "Tag-based cache invalidation"
$Synopsis auto

DESCRIPTION
===========

``cachetag`` records cache-tag membership when an object is stored and
publishes purge history for those tags.  Purge history is the only invalidation
model: a purge never walks object postings and therefore an accepted purge
returns ``-1``.

$Event event_function

$Object namespace(STRING name = "default",
		  INT max_keys_per_object = 512,
		  INT max_key_length = 1024,
		  INT max_tag_header_bytes = 16384,
		  STRING persist_path = "",
		  ENUM { strict, grouped } wal_fsync = strict,
		  INT wal_segment_bytes = 67108864,
		  DURATION sweep_interval = 60,
		  INT purge_history_max_entries = 1000000,
		  INT sweep_batch_objects = 8192,
		  DURATION sweep_batch_hold = 0.00025,
		  DURATION sweep_batch_yield = 0.0001)

Create a cache-tag namespace.

``max_keys_per_object`` bounds the unique tags registered on one object.
Exceeding it during a fetch fails that fetch: tags are never silently
dropped, because delivering an object with a truncated tag set would let a
later purge miss it. Production traces have shown real 333-tag objects, so
the 512 default is sized to pass them; raise it explicitly for workloads
with larger tag sets. ``max_tag_header_bytes`` bounds a single header value:
an oversized ``add_header()`` value fails the fetch the same way, while an
oversized ``purge_header()`` value rejects the whole call with ``-2`` before
any tag is published.

``sweep_interval`` controls the volatile-membership sweep cadence; ``0s``
disables interval sweeps. ``purge_history_max_entries`` bounds retained purge
history when non-zero. Raising a history floor can invalidate old objects, so
production caps should be generous.

``sweep_batch_objects`` and ``sweep_batch_hold`` bound each volatile-membership
sweep batch by object count and elapsed ``obj_mtx`` hold time. ``sweep_batch_yield``
optionally adds a cooperative sleep after releasing ``obj_mtx`` between batches.

``persist_path`` enables durable purge history. The WAL is replayed before the
namespace becomes ready. Fellow objects keep their folded tags and registration
sequence in a checksummed FDO attribute and are probed directly on hit or
deliver; they do not create VMOD object membership. A Fellow allocation that
falls back to transient storage uses volatile membership instead. Missing or
invalid attributes fail closed; a valid envelope without this namespace means
the object has no membership in this namespace.

``wal_fsync=grouped`` is currently a compatibility spelling for ``strict`` and
performs an ``fsync()`` before a purge succeeds. A bounded-window group-commit
implementation may give it distinct behavior in a future format version.

The default one-million-entry history cap bounds resident history for persistent
namespaces. Pruned history is represented by durable hard and soft floors in a
streamed checkpoint, so restart cannot resurrect it. Set a different positive
cap to tune the bound; ``0`` deliberately restores unbounded retention.

$Method VOID .add(STRING key)

Register ``key`` for the object being fetched.

$Restrict vcl_backend_response vcl_backend_error

$Method VOID .add_header(STRING header, STRING sep = ",")

Parse and register tag keys from ``header``. Tokens are trimmed around
``sep`` and must not contain embedded whitespace. There is no independent
per-header token ceiling: the header value is bounded by
``max_tag_header_bytes`` and the unique keys it registers by
``max_keys_per_object``. Exceeding either limit fails the fetch
(fail closed); tags are never silently dropped.

$Restrict vcl_backend_response vcl_backend_error

$Method INT .purge(STRING key, ENUM { hard, soft } mode = hard)

Publish a hard or soft purge for ``key``. An accepted purge returns ``-1``;
``-2`` means a resource or configured limit rejected it, ``-3`` means invalid
input, and ``-4`` means durable persistence is unavailable or failed. A purge
after registration invalidates that registration, while a purge before
registration does not.

$Restrict vcl_recv vcl_hit vcl_miss vcl_pass vcl_deliver vcl_synth vcl_backend_fetch vcl_backend_response vcl_backend_error

$Method INT .purge_header(STRING header, STRING sep = ",",
			  ENUM { hard, soft } mode = hard)

Parse and purge every tag in ``header``. All tokens are validated before any
publication. The token count has no independent ceiling; the header value is
bounded by ``max_tag_header_bytes`` and each token by ``max_key_length``.
Accepted purges return ``-1`` only after every tag is published.
Publication is sequential, so if a later durable publication fails, earlier
tags remain purged and the error is returned. The same negative error codes as
``purge()`` apply.

$Restrict vcl_recv vcl_hit vcl_miss vcl_pass vcl_deliver vcl_synth vcl_backend_fetch vcl_backend_response vcl_backend_error

$Method BOOL .stale()

Return true if the current hit/delivery object has a later hard purge, or is
already leaving the hash. A true result marks the object for removal. A soft
purge reduces matching objects once and returns false.

$Restrict vcl_hit vcl_deliver

$Method INT .pending()

Return unconsumed pending registrations.

$Method INT .objects()

Return live volatile-membership objects. Fellow-direct objects deliberately do
not appear here.

$Method INT .edges()

Return live volatile-membership tag folds. Fellow-direct objects deliberately
do not appear here.

$Method INT .compact()

Prune over-cap purge history, rebuild tombstone-heavy purge maps, perform one
volatile-membership sweep, and request deferred low-water container resize
maintenance. The return value reports logical sweep work; object and side-table
containers converge asynchronously.

$Restrict vcl_recv vcl_hit vcl_miss vcl_pass vcl_deliver vcl_synth vcl_backend_fetch vcl_backend_response vcl_backend_error
